Q2.1
In 64-bit CPython, each list slot stores an 8-byte (64-bit) reference to a Python object.
A cache line is 64 bytes. In the padded version, the stride is 16 elements:
16 × 8 bytes = 128 bytes
Since 128 bytes > 64 bytes, the locations used by neighboring threads are separated by more than one cache-line width. Under the memory-layout assumption used in this benchmark, this prevents neighboring threads from accessing reference slots within the same 64-byte cache line and reduces false sharing.
In my experiment, the median time for adjacent indices was 0.3504 s, while the median time for padded indices was 0.3434 s. The slowdown factor was:
0.3504 / 0.3434 = 1.02x
Therefore, the false-sharing configuration was approximately 1.02 times slower than the padded configuration.
Q2.2
MESI has four cache-line states: Modified, Exclusive, Shared, and Invalid.
When Core 0 and Core 1 initially have access to the same cache line, the line may be in the Shared (S) state. When Core 0 writes to index 0, it needs exclusive ownership of the cache line. Other copies are invalidated and Core 0 changes its copy to the Modified (M) state.
When Core 1 later writes to index 1 located on the same cache line, Core 1 must also obtain ownership. A cache-coherence invalidation is generated. Core 0's copy changes from Modified to Invalid (I), while Core 1 obtains the line and changes it to Modified (M).
When the two cores repeatedly write to different elements on the same cache line, ownership of the cache line moves between the cores. This creates cache-coherence traffic and pipeline stalls even though the threads are modifying different variables. This effect is called false sharing.
Q2.3
Two industry-standard mechanisms used to reduce false sharing are C++ alignas(64) and Java @Contended.
In C++, alignas(64) can align an object or structure to a 64-byte boundary. This helps place frequently modified data on separate cache lines, reducing unnecessary cache invalidations between threads.
In Java, @Contended allows the JVM to add padding around selected fields or classes. This separates frequently updated values in memory and reduces the possibility that different threads modify data located on the same cache line.
These mechanisms are better than manually adding unused array elements because the compiler or runtime can manage memory alignment and padding in a more systematic way.
